Reused evidence
None beyond the local priors supplied for 2022.

New evidence
Macro and stabilization
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Completes 2022 Article IV Mission to Thailand","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/06/13/pr22195-imf-staff-completes-2022-article-iv-mission-to-thailand","claim":"The IMF said Thailand was gradually recovering from the COVID-19 downturn, projected about 3 percent growth in 2022, described inflation as rising, and said policy support should become more targeted while monetary normalization remain data-driven.","locator":"key paragraphs on recovery, inflation, and policy response","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous IMF mission statement based on official discussions with the authorities and a dated macro assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 policy environment and outlook.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut government as the incumbent executive during the mission; much of the recovery also reflects reopening and external conditions.","contrary_evidence":["The same statement says the outlook was highly uncertain and inflation was driven by global energy prices, limiting ruler credit."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Completes 2022 Article IV Mission to Thailand","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-06-13","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/06/13/pr22195-imf-staff-completes-2022-article-iv-mission-to-thailand","claim":"The IMF welcomed plans to phase out broad-based energy subsidies, support vulnerable groups, and prioritize productivity-enhancing spending in education, ICT, green-resilient infrastructure, and social protection, while also urging household-debt restructuring.","locator":"key paragraphs on targeted support, fiscal consolidation, and structural reform","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same IMF mission statement, but this passage adds distinct policy guidance on spending quality and debt management.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 policy choices and the near-term policy mix.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to the Prayut government because it describes government policy options and implementation priorities, not a single personal order.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF is advising the authorities, so the passage does not prove implementation success by itself."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.7","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand: 2022 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; Staff Report; and Statement by the Executive Director for Thailand","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-09-01","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/09/01/pr22299-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-thailand","claim":"The IMF backed near-term fiscal consolidation, said untargeted energy subsidies should be gradually replaced with targeted support to vulnerable groups, and said enhancing domestic revenue mobilization would help rebuild policy buffers.","locator":"Executive Board Assessment and fiscal-policy paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF Executive Board conclusion with dated policy recommendations and a formal country report context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 fiscal policy and macro management.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut administration's policy mix, though the text is IMF advice rather than a direct decree.","contrary_evidence":["The passage is normative advice, so it should not be read as proof that Prayut himself authored the policy path."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand's economy is projected to recover to its pre-pandemic level in 2022","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-14","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/14/thailand-s-economy-remains-resilient-amid-global-headwinds","claim":"The World Bank said Thailand's economy recovered to pre-pandemic level in 2022, growth accelerated to 4.5 percent in Q3 on private consumption and tourism, and the government largely maintained proactive fiscal policy to support recovery and cost-of-living relief.","locator":"key findings and fiscal-policy bullets","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank Thailand Economic Monitor press release with specific dated findings and policy characterizations.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mixed attribution: part reflects Prayut-era fiscal choices, while the rebound also reflects reopening and tourism normalization.","contrary_evidence":["The same release notes the recovery was driven by reopening and global conditions, not just domestic policy."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand's economy is projected to recover to its pre-pandemic level in 2022","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-14","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/14/thailand-s-economy-remains-resilient-amid-global-headwinds","claim":"The World Bank projected poverty to rise to 6.6 percent in 2022 as relief measures were phased out amid elevated inflation and said more targeted and cost-effective social assistance was needed.","locator":"poverty and distribution paragraphs","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same release, but this is a distinct distributional claim with a clear numerical forecast and policy implication.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 outcomes and late-2022 policy conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut government only in the sense that its fiscal choices shaped the safety net; inflation and commodity shocks also mattered.","contrary_evidence":["The passage is a forecast and recommendation, not a direct ruling on government intent or implementation."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rural Thailand Faces the Largest Poverty Challenges with High Income Inequality","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-21","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/10/21/rural-thailand-faces-the-largest-poverty-challenges-with-high-income-inequality","claim":"The World Bank said 79 percent of Thailand's poor remained in rural areas, poverty reduction slowed from 2015 onward, and 70 percent of rural households reported lower income since March 2020.","locator":"opening paragraphs and diagnostic summary","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank diagnostic summary with direct statistics and a dated launch context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2022 and describing the inherited distributional structure affecting 2022 policy.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly inherited and structural; it limits the degree to which Prayut can be credited for overall welfare outcomes.","contrary_evidence":["Because the figures are structural, they do not isolate Prayut-specific policy failure or success in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bridging the Gap: Inequality and Jobs in Thailand","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-10-11","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/thailand/publication/bridging-the-gap-inequality-and-jobs-in-thailand","claim":"The World Bank said Thailand's income Gini was 43.3 percent in 2021, the highest in East Asia and Pacific, and that over half of national wealth was held by the richest 10 percent.","locator":"key findings","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank country publication using explicit inequality metrics and dated findings.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Close to the 2022 target year and describing the distributional baseline entering 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Primarily inherited and structural; useful as baseline context rather than direct ruler credit or blame.","contrary_evidence":["It does not by itself show what Prayut changed in 2022."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country Private Sector Diagnostic for Thailand: Rebooting Productivity for Resilient Growth","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/thailand/publication/country-private-sector-diagnostic-for-thailand-rebooting-productivity-for-resilient-growth?cid=ECR_YT_Worldbank_EN_EXT","claim":"The World Bank said Thailand needed stronger competition, looser foreign-investment restrictions, better skills, more innovative finance, and a clearer digital regulatory framework to put growth back on track.","locator":"key findings and recommended priorities","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank/IFC diagnostic with concrete structural constraints and policy priorities.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Published during 2022 and describing the investment backdrop Prayut inherited and faced.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly inherited context; it does not prove Prayut personally caused the constraints, but it frames the space for reform.","contrary_evidence":["The report is diagnostic, so it cannot establish whether Prayut implemented or rejected the recommendations."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}
Policy and stability
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bank of Thailand clarifies S&P lowered credit ratings on four Thai commercial banks","publisher":"Bank of Thailand","publication_date":"2022-03-22","url":"https://www.bot.or.th/en/news-and-media/news/news-20220322.html","claim":"The BOT said ratings downgrades reflected increasing household debt and a fragile tourism-led recovery, while its relief programs had helped borrowers and left the banking system resilient overall.","locator":"summary section and banking-stability paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct central-bank release with explicit debt, recovery, and balance-sheet details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut-era policy environment through banking relief and recovery management, but the immediate actor was the central bank.","contrary_evidence":["The same release shows a serious debt overhang and fragile recovery, so resilience was incomplete."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Monetary Policy Committee’s Decision 6/2022","publisher":"Bank of Thailand","publication_date":"2022-11-30","url":"https://www.bot.or.th/en/news-and-media/news/news-20221130-2.html","claim":"The BOT unanimously raised the policy rate from 1.00 to 1.25 percent and said gradual policy normalization was appropriate because the recovery had strengthened and inflation pressures remained elevated.","locator":"decision summary and rationale","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct, dated central-bank policy decision with a clear rate move and justification.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect to Prayut through the policy environment and cabinet oversight; the move itself was made by the independent central bank.","contrary_evidence":["This is monetary policy, so it is only partly attributable to Prayut and partly independent of him."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand approves tax cut on diesel to ease high prices","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-02-15","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/commodities-news/thailand-approves-tax-cut-on-diesel-to-ease-high-prices-2764110","claim":"Thailand's cabinet cut the diesel excise tax by 3 baht per litre for three months to blunt high energy prices and help keep retail fuel prices under 30 baht per litre.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous report with direct fiscal-measure details and clear cabinet attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly to Prayut's cabinet, though the immediate action was taken collectively by ministers rather than by Prayut alone.","contrary_evidence":["The measure was temporary and expensive, so it relieved costs without solving the underlying inflation shock."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Parliament passes $93 billion budget bill at first reading","publisher":"Reuters via The Fiji Times","publication_date":"2022-06-03","url":"https://www.fijitimes.com.fj/thai-parliament-passes-93-billion-budget-bill-at-first-reading/","claim":"Thailand's parliament passed the first reading of a 3.185 trillion baht budget for fiscal 2023 aimed at supporting recovery, and Prayut said it would restore strong, sustained growth, reduce inequality, and disburse aid transparently.","locator":"budget passage and Prayut quotation","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters dispatch with a direct quotation from Prayut and concrete budget figures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and about the budget that governed the next fiscal year.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Prayut because the statement is his and the budget was his government's central fiscal instrument.","contrary_evidence":["The passage reports legislative passage, not budget execution or measured outcomes."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand targets 5 million to 15 million foreign tourists this year","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-05-06","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/thailand-targets-5-million-to-15-million-foreign-tourists-this-year-2819202","claim":"Thailand aimed for 5 million to 15 million foreign tourist arrivals in 2022 after easing travel restrictions, and a meeting chaired by Prayut approved extending domestic-travel support including a 40 percent subsidy on one million room nights.","locator":"opening paragraphs and recovery-support paragraph","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous report with explicit tourism and support-program details plus chair attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exactly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Prayut's government through the cabinet/task-force meeting he chaired, though reopening was also a response to the pandemic.","contrary_evidence":["Tourism recovery depended heavily on reopening conditions and was still far below 2019 levels."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand approves enhanced incentives to boost EV use","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-04-07","url":"https://www.euronews.com/next/2022/04/07/thailand-economy-investment","claim":"Thailand expanded EV incentives, removed some investment barriers, and kept a goal of having 30 percent of total auto output be EVs by 2030.","locator":"key findings and incentive paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on a specific policy package with direct quotes from the investment agency.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut government through the BOI and cabinet policy framework, though the BOI led implementation.","contrary_evidence":["The article describes an investment-promotion package rather than a proven broad welfare gain."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thai government spending to target low-income groups - Finance Minister","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-11-16","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/thai-government-spending-to-target-lowincome-groups--finance-minister-2945409","claim":"Finance Minister Arkhom said stimulus would become more targeted toward vulnerable groups and low-income households as more people returned to work, and that fiscal policy would be more focused.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous policy reporting with direct ministerial attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Prayut government through the finance minister, but the policy statement came from Arkhom rather than Prayut personally.","contrary_evidence":["The same report says stimulus was being reduced, so the measure reflects normalization as well as help for the poor."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand 2022 Investment Pledges up 39% to USD20 Billion Helped by FDI flows in Electronics, EV, Data Centers","publisher":"Thailand Board of Investment via PRNewswire","publication_date":"2023-01-13","url":"https://www.prnewswire.co.uk/news-releases/thailand-2022-investment-pledges-up-39-to-usd20-billion-helped-by-fdi-flows-in-electronics-ev-data-centers-301721247.html","claim":"The BOI said 2022 investment promotion applications reached 664.6 billion baht, up 39 percent, with strong EV, electronics, and data-center inflows, and that a board meeting chaired by Prayut approved a headquarters-facilitation mechanism and renewable-energy steps.","locator":"opening release and board-meeting paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official BOI release syndicated by PRNewswire with specific 2022 investment numbers and named meeting outcomes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 outcomes, published in early 2023 while Prayut remained prime minister.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant because Prayut chaired the board meeting and the outcome summarizes 2022 investment policy results.","contrary_evidence":["The release is a promotional agency statement, so it should be balanced against weaker first-half investment data and broader macro headwinds."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand sees 22% drop in investment pledges this year","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-09-12","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/thailand-sees-22-drop-in-investment-pledges-this-year-2890250","claim":"Thailand expected overall investment applications to fall 22 percent in 2022 after a first-half slump, with foreign investment more than halving year on year, even though EV and digital projects held up better.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on official BOI data with clear year-on-year figures and sector breakdowns.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly attributable to external slowdown rather than Prayut alone, though it still reflects the government's investment climate.","contrary_evidence":["The same report notes EV and digital investment remained comparatively strong, so the investment picture was mixed rather than uniformly weak."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.9","5B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary
I reviewed roughly 25-40 documents across IMF, World Bank, Bank of Thailand, BOI, Reuters-syndicated reporting, Nation Thailand, and a few alternative mirrors or search-result pages. I rejected or deprioritized:
- Duplicate syndications that repeated the same Reuters copy without adding a new material claim.
- Out-of-period material from 2024-2026 that would not help a 2022 chapter dossier.
- Generic country pages and raw data pages that only repeated the supplied local priors.
- Weak or purely promotional pages that did not add ruler-period evidence beyond the official release already retained.
- Some budget/investment pages that were useful only as background and did not add a distinct 2022 claim once the retained budget, tourism, and BOI evidence were in place.

Remaining gaps
- `5B.2` is still thin. I did not find strong ruler-specific evidence that Prayut personally appointed, protected, or empowered economic professionals in a way that can be separated from technocratic agency autonomy.
- `5B.5` remains weak. I found little direct 2022 evidence on corruption, favoritism, or politically connected privilege that tied cleanly to Prayut's personal choices rather than the broader Thai political economy.
- `5B.7` is only partly covered. The record shows targeted policy adjustments and central-bank normalization, but less evidence of explicit mistake-correction by Prayut himself.
- More search would help on cabinet minutes, budget execution, and anti-capture enforcement if you want a sharper attribution picture.
